      excerpt: "Bone shrinks after a tooth is lost. A 3D scan shows how much is left, and whether it needs building up first.",
      body: "After a tooth is removed, the bone that held it slowly shrinks, most in the first year. An implant needs enough bone around it, in height and in width, to stay firm.\n\nA CBCT scan measures this in 3D before anything is planned. If there is too little, a bone graft adds bone-like material to the area, covered by a thin membrane. It usually heals for three to six months before the implant goes in, and sometimes both are done together.\n\nIn the upper back jaw, the sinus sits just above the roots. A sinus lift gently raises the sinus floor and places graft material under it, to make room for the implant.\n\nBoth are done under local anaesthesia. Expect swelling for a few days. They add time and cost, so they should appear in your written estimate from the start, not as a surprise later.",
