      body: "The crown on an implant cannot get a cavity. The gum and bone around it can still become inflamed if plaque is left there, a condition called peri-implantitis. It is the most common reason implants fail years later.\n\nBrush around the implant twice a day, and clean the sides every day with an interdental brush or a water flosser. Under a full-arch bridge, a water flosser or special floss reaches where a brush cannot.\n\nCome in for a review and professional cleaning every six months in the first years. X-rays taken once a year check the bone level around the implant.\n\nSmoking and uncontrolled diabetes raise the risk of problems. Bleeding, swelling or a loose feeling around an implant needs a check soon, not at the next routine visit.",
